(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609110500-the-q9-procedures-other-half-a-doctor-that-fixes-forward")
  (:adr/topic "kotoba-doctor-fix-forward")
  (:adr/title "The Q9 procedure's other half: a doctor that fixes forward, and two ways of judging a repair that throw away correct ones")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609101900-q9-rename-reverted-pending-admission"
                        "adr-2609110300-kotoba-adl-convert-asks-whether-a-clojure-consumer-remains"
                        "adr-2609110400-the-converters-reader-was-more-permissive-than-the-compilers"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

Owner instruction 2026-09-10, restating a standing one: **rename to .kotoba
FIRST, then fix with build, compile, doctor.**

I had started in the opposite direction -- adding an `amu check` gate in front
of `kotoba adl convert`, so nothing would be renamed unless the compiler would
already accept it. That is a converter that refuses to convert, and it is not
the owner's procedure. Recorded because the instinct was strong and wrong: the
previous two ADRs both LOWERED the convertible count on purpose, and a third
lowering felt like continuity rather than the drift it was.

## What was actually missing

Measured 2026-09-10: `kotoba` had exactly ONE subcommand, `adl`. `amu` has no
doctor either. So the second half of the owner's procedure had no tool at all
-- a renamed tree could be diagnosed one file at a time by hand, and repaired
not at all.

That is the gap ADR-2609101900 fell into. Twenty repositories were renamed,
`amu check` could say why each of nineteen files failed, and the only actions
available were to read nineteen answers by hand or revert. It was reverted.

## What was measured

The closure of those twenty repositories -- 29 source files, copied out,
renamed to .kotoba, checked in project mode against amu 94e9c29b:

    29 refused. Nineteen of them with ONE message:
      :kotoba/project-link-failed  project module requires an explicit :export vector
       5  only one :export, one :capabilities and alias-only :require clauses
       2  source reader rejected input
       2  imports require [namespace :as alias]
       1  namespace docstring exceeds admission limit

This reproduces ADR-2609101900's four classes on a wider corpus (29 vs 19
files) and adds a fifth. The dominant class is mechanical.

## Decision

`kotoba doctor <paths> [--source-path <dir>] [--fix]`. Diagnose by running
`amu check` over the tree and grouping failures by the code and message amu
itself returns -- the tally IS the work list. With `--fix`, apply the
mechanical repairs and re-run amu.

Result on the same corpus:

    14 repaired    the :export class 19 -> 5
    the tree now shows THIRTEEN distinct defects instead of one wall:
      protocol-declaration, variadic parameters, count-receiver,
      unbound-symbol, parameter-use-conflict, unknown-operation, ...

The five remaining each have a public `def` of a VALUE. amu requires an export
to name a declared function and says so exactly -- `answer is a def constant,
not a function; export a function that returns it`. The repair refuses those
by name rather than exporting only the functions. Two refusals are load-bearing
and both are the same rule:

  **A repair never narrows the module's public surface to satisfy a check.**
  `(:export [])` would pass amu's presence test while publishing nothing, and
  a module that links because it exports less than its source did is the shape
  Q9's whole-component rule exists to forbid.

## Two ways of judging a repair, both of which threw away correct ones

Worth recording because both looked like the careful choice.

**Judging on whether the FILE came out clean kept 0 of 14.** Adding
`(:export [union])` to kotoba.set.union genuinely admits the clause; amu then
refuses the module further in for a variadic parameter -- a second, unrelated
defect the export repair was never going to reach. Requiring a clean file
means only modules with exactly one defect can ever be repaired, which is not
fixing forward. Judged on whether the CLASS THIS REPAIR TARGETS moved, the
same code keeps 14. What keeps that honest is the other arm: if the finding is
unchanged, the edit did nothing and is undone.

**Judging file-by-file then cost 3 more.** In project mode amu links the whole
graph, so a module is refused because a module it REQUIRES is refused. The
sorted walk reaches datalog/index.kotoba before datom/source.kotoba, so index
was repaired, re-checked against an unrepaired dependency, and reverted.

⚠ `:source` does not separate those. Measured: with index repaired and its
dependency not, amu answers `:source \"index.kotoba\"` -- the ENTRY it was asked
about, not the module actually missing the vector. So ordering is solved where
it belongs: every repair is applied BEFORE any is re-checked, and a third pass
re-measures the whole tree. The counts reported are that measurement, not a
deduction from the first two passes.

## Controls

Four mutations, each caught by exactly the test that names the behaviour:
emitting `(:export [])`; a verdict that always says progressed; privacy by
text scan instead of CST (the negative half is `^:private` inside a
DOCSTRING, which must exclude nothing); and dropping the bytes after the
insertion point.

⚠ **The fourth passed every test.** Every fixture happened to end its ns form
flush against the closing paren, so there were no trailing bytes to drop. A
fixture with trivia between the last clause and the paren -- the shape
kotoba.set.union actually has -- turns it red. A control suite that covers
only the shapes you thought of is the same green as one with nothing to find.

At the CLI, refusing and passing are different exit codes and both were
exercised: 0 files scanned, `$AMU` unset with amu off PATH, and `$AMU` pointing
at nothing all exit 2. A tree amu admits exits 0 with no findings -- so a
finding here is a measurement and not a habit.

## What this does NOT claim

**It does not move admission on that corpus: 0 of 29 files are admitted.** It
moves the WORK LIST -- from one message repeated nineteen times to thirteen
named defects, of which the language-level ones (variadic parameters, count
receivers, unannotated parameters whose uses disagree) are decisions and not
edits a tool should make.

It does not resume the rename. ADR-2609101900's three options are still the
owner's to choose. What changes is that option 1 -- fix admission first, then
re-rename -- now has a tool for the mechanical two thirds of it.

It says nothing about `kotoba adl convert`'s refusals. The build guard still
refuses a partial conversion of a source root, and should: a rename does not
fail, and nbb resolves namespaces by extension.

## Landed

kotoba-lang/kotoba c4ff5ca71f43 (PR #618). Repairs are portable `.cljc` in
`kotoba.doctor`; only tree-walking and the subprocess are in the nbb driver.
`test/kotoba/doctor_test.cljc`, 8 tests / 22 assertions, registered in
`run-tests.cljs`. west pin 47aa4de0 -> c4ff5ca7.

⚠ That runner needs the full `clojure -Spath -M:test` classpath and fails on
`did.core` without it -- verified identical on origin/main's own copy, so it is
pre-existing and not this change. The new test has no external dependency and
runs standalone.")))
